    "artist": "Laura Ormiston-Chant",
    "credit": "The Gaiety Theatre, Harris Promenade, Douglas, Isle of Man",
    "source": "The Gaiety Theatre, Harris Promenade, Douglas, Isle of Man",
    "description": "The Gaiety Theatre and Opera House is a theatre, on the Isle of Man. The theatre was built in 1899 to the designs of architect Frank Matcham, as an opera house and theatre.  It was built within the structure of the former Pavilion, an entertainment hall that had been constructed six years earlier.\nThe theatre opened on 16 July 1900 with a West End production of The Telephone Girl featuring Ada Blanche. The theatre enjoyed considerable success in the Edwardian era, but after World War I the island's tourist industry fell into decline, and so did the theatre.\nVarious attempts were made to regain its former commercial success, including the 1920s installation of cinema equipment and a 1938 ice show. Income could not keep pace with the cost of maintenance, and by 1971 the Isle of Man government acquired the freehold of the now dilapidated building. In 1976 the restoration began under the direction of architect Victor Glasstone.\nThe theatre has undergone further restoration beginning in the 1990s and was largely restored to its 1900 opening condition, in time for the centenary celebration in 2000. Exactly 100 years after opening on July 16, 2000, the centenary was celebrated with a performance of \"The Telephone Girl\" which opened the Gaiety in 1900. Following that came a performance of \"The Corsican Brothers\", a play which was popular in Victorian times and a special Corsican Trap was constructed for that performance. It is believed to be the only working Corsican Trap in the World.\nAnother unique feature of the theatre is the working Act Drop depicting a dancing lady.\nToday the theatre continues with productions by local companies and touring productions of musicals, drama and opera. It now forms a part of the Villa Marina complex, together with a nearby 1,500 seat auditorium.\nThe Gaiety theatre featured on an Isle of Man 24p commemorative stamp, in 1994.\n\nIn February 2008, The Gaiety played host to Hollywood movie Me and Orson Welles, starring Zac Efron and Claire Danes. The Gaiety replicates the Mercury Theatre in New York in 1937.",
    "dateCreated": "2013-07-27 09:53",
    "categories": [
      "CC-BY-2.0",
      "Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation (54° N, 5° W)",
